    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "33-36",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Blue Jays keep living on the edge and paying the bill - and last night's <strong>4-7 loss to the Phillies</strong> was another reminder that this is a club stuck between promise and drag. At 33-36, third in the AL East and 9 games back, they're not out of the chase, but they're running out of runway; the 8th seed keeps the door cracked, not wide open. The encouraging counterpoint is that the lineup has shown some bite in this Phillies stretch - the Jays won 3-2 on Monday, then dropped the next one, which is the kind of up-and-down rhythm that defines a middling team more than a contender. Injuries haven't been the loudest storyline here; execution has. Too many innings drift, too many rallies die, and too often the margin is a crooked number the other way. Still, the calendar offers a clean test. <strong>Friday night's meeting with the Yankees in Toronto will say plenty about whether this club can stiffen its spine</strong> - or keep looking like a team that can trade punches but not land the finishing one.",
        "updated": "Updated June 11, 2026"
      },
