      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Commanders",
        "body": "The offseason has a pulse again in Ashburn, and it is beating through the tight ends and the quarterback room. <strong>Washington's OTA buzz has shifted toward how quickly Chig Okonkwo, Ben Sinnott and John Bates are absorbing new coordinator David Blough's offense</strong> - a telltale sign that this scheme wants to live in 12 and 13 personnel and bully defenses with formation, not flash. That matters for a club sitting third in the NFC East, with a 3-6 mark, six games back and parked at playoff seed 14; the record says work remains, but the calendar says the real fight is for roles, not results. The bigger question hanging over camp is the one every team answers in June and regrets in September: who owns the snaps? The reports out of practice suggest the tight ends are ahead of schedule, while Jayden Daniels is still syncing timing and cadence in the new system. That is where this gets interesting - an offense built on rhythm can only hum if the quarterback and his new toys are in tune. <strong>Watch the next wave of minicamp reps for the pecking order at tight end and how Daniels settles the passing tempo</strong>.",
